  "worked_solution": "From the graph of the function $f(y),$ we can deduce the following information:\n\n- The solutions to $f(y)=0$ are $y=-1$ and $y=1.$ Therefore $y=-1,1$ are the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation.\n\n- $f(y)>0$ when $y\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\n- $f(y)<0$ when $y\\in (-1,1).$\n\nTherefore, we can draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14042698/assets/fc4cd58a2c1a6e8147099455ca2439a9d7be7eb39c54f12387061bb6305e8f74.png)",
